udience you have one that  is incredibly narrow and is basically  touching one two three four five six  seven other districts this little sliver  of land and then you see another  district and  yellow in the northeastern part of the  state which sticks out into very  specific cities and towns in a way that  again is making the surrounding district  more white and more likely to all go  Republican and concentrating the black  voters within these two districts so  this is great but again this is a map  that was drawn after the 2010 census its  2017 that means that these lines have  been in effect for years a lot of damage  has been done these districts were in  place in 2012 in 2014 in 2016 as far as  the federal level in intra North  Carolina elections  additionally beyond that and that's part  of the problem right North Carolina  Republicans drew these lines in 2011  they were probably aware that they were  doing something that was questionable  legally but they know it's going to take  in this particular case it was six years  we will get a number of congressional  and state legislative wins before the  Supreme Court if they ever do will make  a decision about this they still won and  that's why the US Census that's coming  up in a few years is so important that's  why we need to reform our process when  it comes to drawing district lines and  of course gerrymandering takes three  different forms as packing stacking and  cracking yeah I think this is a clear  example of packing because if you're  able to put the african-american  communities into two distinct districts  then of course they're going to be very  influential in those districts but as  part of the state overall  not so much state legislatures can  consider race when drawing lines if it  helps them comply with the federal  Voting Rights Act but Republicans were  doing the opposite here right they were  drawing lines based on race in order to  silence and disenfranchise black voters  and these discriminatory lines are in  addition to constant attempts by North  Carolina Republicans to pass voter ID  laws that would suppress black votes  last week the Supreme Court ruled that  the strict voter ID laws proposed by  North Carolina Republicans were  unconstitutional and unfairly harm  minorities so it has been a good week  for voting rights in North Carolina more  than seven years after the  census that led to this in the first  place for now it is I mean the problem  here is that it comes down to a judges  interpretation some of these states and  jurisdictions have laws where these  districts have to be compact and  contiguous yeah but outside of that  there's not much regulation when it  comes to this sort of thing and the  mindset is well I know a good district  when I see one when he has a lot of  these judges and obviously different  judges have different political leanings  of yesterday day I to pretend that they  don't yes they do and to all the Bernie  or Buster's or the never Hillary people  out there this is why Supreme Court  appointments matter what are the chances  that Donald Trump cares anything about  appointing Supreme Court justices who  would rule against this kind of racial  gerrymandering his record on voter  suppression suggests that he wouldn't  care about that and this is exactly why  we need to be aware of all of the  repercussions of these sort of  Manichaean black-white perspectives on  never this or always that or whatever  these are multi-generational impacts  that happen as a result of who's on the  Supreme Court yeah when it came to the  2016 election a lot of people were just  focusing on Trump's past and Hillary's  past their expertise in different forms  and their reputations it matters a lot  more you know who gets the majorities in  Congress who is going to go on Supreme  Court all these other things matter  no probably more absolutely we've got a  great bonus show planned for you today  the American Legion calls on Donald  Trump to 